    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d945">

                <pb facs="0989" n="869" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg869"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00 Bandit Activities, 1931/237:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Nicaragua</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Beaulac</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-17T14:00:00-04:00">September 17, 1931—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>175. American Consul, Bluefields, telegraphed September 11<anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d945-f1"/> he has informed you of reported death sentences imposed
                    on two bandits following court martial by Guardia. See Department’s January 16,
                    1930, 6 p.m., January 17, 1930, 5 p.m. and January 29, 4 p.m., and <pb
                        facs="0990" n="870" xml:id="pg870"/> endeavor to see to it that death
                    sentences if imposed are not carried out.</p>
                <p>Advise what action subsequent to Legation’s No. 1328, February 18, 1930, has been
                    taken by Nicaraguan Government to establish civil courts adequate to deal with
                    cases involving banditry.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
